WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.380
Blood or tissue holds a mixture of cells.

2
00:00:03.720 --> 00:00:06.440
How do you count each kind, or pull one group out pure?

3
00:00:08.040 --> 00:00:10.940
Bulk tests average them, hiding what single cells do.

4
00:00:13.983 --> 00:00:15.703
First, DNA content.

5
00:00:17.163 --> 00:00:18.863
One C is one haploid genome.

6
00:00:20.323 --> 00:00:23.603
S phase, between G1 and G2, copies the DNA.

7
00:00:24.903 --> 00:00:29.823
So a diploid cell holds 2C in G1, 4C in G2 and mitosis.

8
00:00:31.403 --> 00:00:36.103
Stain fixed cells with propidium iodide, a fluorescent dye for nucleic acid.

9
00:00:37.283 --> 00:00:40.143
Add RNase, because the dye binds RNA too.

10
00:00:41.217 --> 00:00:43.657
Cells stream one at a time past a laser.

11
00:00:44.997 --> 00:00:50.397
Each flash builds a histogram: a 2C peak, a 4C peak, and S phase between.

12
00:00:51.717 --> 00:00:55.797
The rule: read fluorescent tags on single cells to count or sort.

13
00:00:57.417 --> 00:01:00.337
Swap in fluorescent antibodies, and tagged cells separate.

14
00:01:01.450 --> 00:01:02.930
Look at that histogram.

15
00:01:04.150 --> 00:01:08.890
Resting G0 cells also hold 2C; mitotic cells hold 4C.

16
00:01:10.150 --> 00:01:15.650
Can DNA fluorescence alone separate G0 from G1, or mitosis from G2?

17
00:01:21.200 --> 00:01:24.480
No: DNA content alone has two blind spots.

18
00:01:25.640 --> 00:01:30.620
A short EdU pulse tags newly made DNA, lifting S phase off both peaks.

19
00:01:31.920 --> 00:01:37.740
An antibody to phosphorylated histone H3 marks mitotic chromosomes, starting in late

20
00:01:37.740 --> 00:01:42.200
G2. Still, no single stain marks G0 cleanly.

21
00:01:43.233 --> 00:01:45.213
Controls keep results honest.

22
00:01:46.593 --> 00:01:51.453
Comparing two cell lines, flow cytometry first checks matched amounts of the tagged

23
00:01:51.453 --> 00:01:55.533
protein. Amount alone can change how cells clump.

24
00:01:56.633 --> 00:02:01.013
When nuclei are sorted for a bulk test, stray nuclei skew the average.

25
00:02:02.213 --> 00:02:05.153
And counting immune cells never measures protection.

26
00:02:06.317 --> 00:02:08.197
Sorting unlocked cell ages.

27
00:02:09.857 --> 00:02:14.757
Weapons tests in the 1950s raised carbon-14 in the air, and it later fell.

28
00:02:16.097 --> 00:02:20.417
A neuron stops dividing, so its DNA keeps that year's carbon-14.

29
00:02:21.757 --> 00:02:27.857
In 2005, Spalding and colleagues sorted nuclei with an antibody to NeuN, a neuron

30
00:02:27.857 --> 00:02:32.777
marker. Occipital cortex neurons were as old as the person.

31
00:02:33.933 --> 00:02:37.193
The same sorting told a different story in the hippocampus.

32
00:02:38.653 --> 00:02:45.073
In 2013, Spalding and colleagues fitted hippocampal carbon-14 to turnover models.

33
00:02:46.413 --> 00:02:50.433
They estimated about one third of hippocampal neurons are exchanged.

34
00:02:51.633 --> 00:02:55.193
That is about 700 new ones per hippocampus per day.

35
00:02:56.413 --> 00:03:00.813
The estimate rests on pure sorting, the model, and little DNA repair.

36
00:03:01.950 --> 00:03:07.530
In blood, a fluorescent viral spike protein tags the memory B cells that bind it.

37
00:03:08.730 --> 00:03:16.470
In 2021, Dan and colleagues sampled 188 people up to eight months after SARS-CoV-2

38
00:03:16.470 --> 00:03:17.470
infection.

39
00:03:18.083 --> 00:03:21.963
Plasma spike antibody was fairly stable, but drifted down.

40
00:03:23.323 --> 00:03:27.903
Yet spike-specific memory B cells were more abundant at six months than at one.

41
00:03:29.203 --> 00:03:32.003
One cell at a time reveals what averages hide.
